stors 150V \u00b1100ppm/\u2103 \u00b15% 24\u03a9 0805 Chip Resistor\"}, {\"source\": \"spice\", \"quote\": \"R36 VOUTN VOUTP 24k\"}], \"impact\": \"The value used in simulation and the value on the BOM differ from the physical part. A 24k resistor in place of 24 \u03a9 would change the load or bleed on the VOUTP/VOUTN rail by three orders of magnitude.\", \"confidence\": 0.45}, {\"category\": \"other\", \"components\": [\"U6\", \"Q1\"], \"pins\": [\"U6.1\", \"U6.6\", \"Q1.2\", \"Q1.3\"], \"nets\": [\"USBC_VBUS_IN\", \"+5V\"], \"observed\": \"The LTC4412 VIN pin (U6.1) and the P-MOSFET drain (Q1.3, D) are both on USBC_VBUS_IN. The SENSE pin (U6.6) and the P-MOSFET source (Q1.2, S) are both on +5V. The PMOS body diode therefore points from the +5V rail toward the USB input, the reverse of the intended ideal-diode direction.\", \"expected\": \"For an LTC4412 ideal-diode OR-ing stage, the P-MOSFET source should be on the controller's VIN (the higher-priority input), with the drain on the load/SENSE side, so the body diode conducts only in the intended direction.\", \"evidence\": [{\"source\": \"netlist\", \"quote\": \"(node (ref \\\"Q1\\\") (pin \\\"3\\\") (pinfunction \\\"D\\\") (pintype \\\"passive\\\"))\"}, {\"source\": \"netlist\", \"quote\": \"(node (ref \\\"Q1\\\") (pin \\\"2\\\") (pinfunction \\\"S\\\") (pintype \\\"passive\\\"))\"}, {\"source\": \"spec\", \"quote\": \"USB diode doesn't work, I guess need ideal diode to handle more power? LTC4412 controller and p-ch mosfet. need reverse current protection mostly\"}], \"impact\": \"Reverse-current protection may not work, since the body diode can conduct from the +5V rail back toward USB. This may be the documented 'USB diode doesn't work' issue, so confidence is low.\", \"confidence\": 0.25}], \"summary\": \"The netlist contains two clear passive value inconsistencies: C13 is marked 0.1mF in a 0603 footprint with a 100nF part, and R36 is marked 24k while its MPN and description say 24 \u03a9. The LTC4412/Q1 orientation is a lower-confidence concern because the spec already documents this stage as problematic.\"}",
